  "query_preview": "/THURSDAY THROUGH TUESDAY/...\nIssued at 216 PM CDT Tue Aug 25 2026\n\nThursday into Saturday, upper ridge over the Central CONUS will \ncontinue to slowly shift easterly and flatten out due to waves \npassing through the north. This will result in a period of surface \nhigh pressure, leading to quiet and seasonal weather. \nTemperatures will remain in the 80s, with lower humidity and \nmostly clear skies. Winds will also remain light and variable \nthrough this timeframe, bringing quite the pleasant end to the \nwork week.\n\nSaturday and beyond, the upper ridge finally gets pushed east of the \narea. This shift in the pattern would guide llvl southerly to \nsouthwesterly flow into the region, allowing for temperatures and \nhumidity to increase. Upper level flow will generally be zonal to \nsouthwesterly as well, which will put us under the path of passing \nshortwaves. Thus, if this pattern holds true, it would seem like we \nare heading into a potentially active stretch of weather late this \nweekend and into next week. Global models are in general agreement \non chances for precipitation, with some differences in the timing \nand track of these shortwaves. Thus, some things will need to get \nironed out as we get closer. Although, NBM does introduce daily PoPs \n(20-40%) Sunday into the middle of next week. Too soon to determine \nany severe threat.",
